# HOW MUCH OF THE SYSTEM EACH STACK CLAIMS TO HOLD. One row per filing.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ A FILING IS NEVER THE SYSTEM. There is one system; a filing holds the layers of it
#    that mattered to whoever filed. Reading a stack as an enumeration of the system is what
#    turns a second document's extra layer into an accusation that the first omitted
#    something, when it is two projections of one thing.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND THE THIRD EXTENT IS THE ONE A TWO-VALUED ENCODING WOULD CRUSH. `scoped` says
#    somebody established what lies outside and excluded it on purpose. `unbounded` says
#    nobody looked. Rendered identically, a bounded selection is indistinguishable from an
#    unexamined one -- the same distinction the coupling search draws one level down.
#
# ⛔ `complete` IS FALSIFIABLE IN PRINCIPLE AND BY NOTHING IN THIS CORPUS. What would refute
#   it is another filing holding a layer OF THE SAME SYSTEM that this one lacks -- and no
#   document names a system, only a filing. So it belongs in the owed list rather than in a
#   query that looks as though it checks something.
-- pm:Stack/pm:scope, with its pm:basis.
SELECT ss.filing, ss.extent, ss.basis, ss.absent
FROM pm.stack_scope ss
